Err..  you lost me.  You suggest to translate the game to chinese or 
another language supporting morphem-oriented letters and claim this 
would make the game /easier/ for newbies?  Maybe I am overlooking something, 
but I have the feeling that learning a new language (or at least a couple 
hundred kanji) would be somehow kind of offsetting the claimed advantage 
of more "meaningful" letters.  

> This whole idea makes me wonder, why roguelikes were not originally
> created in China or Japan. Perhaps more propably Japan, since I would
> think they had more computers in the 1980's.

Simple.  Programmers inventend those early games.  Programmers thought 
in C (or worse, assembler), C used roman letters, everything in the 
early computer industry was based on ASCII, and so on.  Kanji support 
was very scattered and absolutely lacking of standards, and hence 
could not be used in writing "generic" code.  

> 1) How are you able to use chinese with a computer? I don't know much
> about chinese, but I've been wondering for years that if you have a
> gazzillion differrent characters, how do you use a keyboard to get them
> in?

Applied Combinatorics.  First, for what I know, they use simplified 
Mandarin (much less kanji), second, they can build up kanji gradually 
(most complex kanji are combinations of some simple basic forms), third, 
they employ a lot of Meta characters (similar to our shift key), fourth, 
they need and get special training.  

> 2) Do you have lower-case and upper-case characters? 

I'd say no.  However, they use different words for counting different 
things (IIRC, at least in Japan, you use different words for "three" 
in the translation of "three sticks" and "three horses").  Gazillions 
of more subtle translation problems abound, I am sure, but my 
knowledge is much too limited (and maybe obfuscated, so CMIIW).  

